<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="(3E)-4,8-dimethylnona-1,3,7-triene biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5434" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The two acyclic homoterpenes |FRAME:CPD-8844| (DMNT) and |FRAME:CPD-8846| (TMTT) are widespread constituents of flower fragrances |CITS:[boland89],[gabler91],[donath95]|. These homoterpenes are released from the foliage of many plant species following herbivore damage |CITS:[boland92],[dicke94]|. These compounds are part of the arsenal used in plant tritrophic &apos;indirect defense&apos;, which uses the volatile compounds to attract predators of the herbivores and help them locate their prey so that they do less damage to the host plants |CITS:[Dicke99],[10517823],[16723271]|. It has been suggested that |FRAME:CPD-8844 DMNT| might be stored as a &amp;beta;-glucoside of its sesquiterpenoid precursor |FRAME:CPD-12568| and is cleaved by a &amp;beta;-glucosidase contained in the regurgitant of the feeding insect. |FRAME:CPD-8844 DMNT| would then be generated by an oxidative degradation as exemplified in this pathway. Synthesis of sesquiterpenes occurs in the cytosol, however, several studies have shown that the isoprene units of |FRAME:CPD-8844 DMNT| originate both from the cytosolic |FRAME:PWY-922| and the plastidic |FRAME:NONMEVIPP-PWY| |CITS:[15796607],[16580034]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP FPP| isoprenoid precursor to this pathway is first acted on by a terpene synthase to form nerolidol. Two different enantiomers may be formed. Different plant species have been observed to contain one or both of these enantiomers (see refs in |CITS: [DONATH95]|) and plant enzymes that produce both the |FRAME:CPD-8843 R-enantiomer| |CITS: [12481088]| and the |FRAME:CPD-12568 S-enantiomer| |CITS: [10805454][10482672]| have been experimentally identified. The next step in the pathway is an oxidative degradation. This was long hypothesized to occur through the activity of a cytochrome p450 monooxygenase, but experimental proof was lacking until an Arabidopsis p450 enzyme, |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1|, was discovered that can catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD-8844 DMNT| from |FRAME: CPD-12568| when expressed in yeast |CITS: [21088219]|. |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1| is unlikely to perform this function &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; because |FRAME: ORG-5993| does not appear to have a nerolidol synthase. However, several potential orthologs of this enzyme in DMNT-producing species may be good candidates to test for DMNT synthase activty. For example, poplar produces DMNT |CITS: [14756770]| and has a member of the CYP82 family of enzymes, CYP82L2 with 53% amino acid identity to |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1||CITS: [21088219]|. Although |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1| acted on the |FRAME:CPD-12568| and a number of other plant species, such as |FRAME: TAX-3884 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Phaseolus lunatus&lt;/i&gt; (lima bean)&quot;|, exclusively act on the S-enantiomer, a broad survey of plants revealed that there are examples of species where both enantiomers can be used |CITS: [DONATH95]|. And a direct test revealed that the |FRAME:CPD-8843 R-enantiomer| can be taken up and used by |FRAME: TAX-4577 maize| to produce |FRAME:CPD-8844 DMNT| |CITS: [12481088]|. Although there is now firm evidence that a cytochrome p450 enzyme can catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD-8844 DMNT| the exact mechanism of enzyme action is still being investigated. For instance, it is possible that |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1| may promote the direct transformation of |FRAME: CPD-12568| to |FRAME: CPD-8844 DMNT| with the concomittant release of |FRAME: CPD-8847| as depicted in this pathway diagram. However, this reaction is labeled a &quot;hypothetical&quot; because this has not been experimentally verified. Interestingly, the formation of |FRAME: CPD-8847| has not been detected in reactions using an analogous compound |FRAME: CPD-8846 TMTT| when |FRAME: AT3G25180-MONOMER CYP82G1| is expressed in yeast (see |FRAME: PWY-6668 TMTT biosynthesis|)|CITS: [21088219]|. The authors state that |FRAME: CPD-8847| may not be detected because of its high chemical reactivity. However, it is also possible that a two-step conversion may occur that includes the formation of intermediate C-13 compound |FRAME: CPD-12901|. This compound has been detected to exist in numerous plant species such as apple and |FRAME: TAX-183823| |CITS:[20860027][20658674]|. Several researchers have examined this |FRAME: CPD-12568| to |FRAME: CPD-8844 DMNT| chemical conversion in different plants (|CITS: [DONATH95]| and see refs in |CITS: [21088219]|), but further work will be required to clarify the exact manner in which the final step of this pathway is executed. Interestingly, a second pathway for the production of |FRAME: CPD-8844| has been discovered in the roots of |FRAME: TAX-3702| - see |FRAME: PWY-6007|.</Description>
